From 452ce0c86892ad3c52da4d068c5a36c42f0b9174 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 08:07:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] State and enforce the restart contract, item 4-3a

The tiering pilot's bailout is RESTART, not resume: a failed guard re-enters
the unoptimized function from the top with the original arguments. There is
nothing to resume into -- a JavaScript local is a CLR local of the compiled
method -- so restart is the only transfer this engine can express, and it is
sound only under three conditions:

  1. every guard fires before any observable effect, because restart re-runs
     the function from the top;
  2. the bailout leaves no CallFrameStack slot behind;
  3. the body is not suspendable, because a generator or async body may already
     have yielded and re-entering it would re-run the effects before the
     suspension point.

All three already held. Conditions 1 and 2 hold for a good reason: the
specialized body touches nothing but its own locals, and it never pushes a
frame (the push lives inside the compiled baseline), so on bailout the baseline
pushes exactly once. Both are now stated on NumericLoopPlan.Compile so a future
plan that breaks either knows it has to use an in-method branch instead.

Condition 3 held by ACCIDENT, twice over, and neither accident is about
speculation. EnableTiering is called inside CreateFunction's ordinary-function
`else` branch, so generators and async functions never reach it -- branch
placement, not a rule. And the tiering gate requires CanScalarReplaceLocals,
which TryPlanScalarReplacement refuses for Async || Generator for its own
reasons. Both are the kind of thing a reasonable refactor removes: hoisting the
call out of the branch is a tidy-up, and teaching scalar replacement about
state-machine fields is a plausible optimization.

So the hazard was measured rather than argued. With both accidental exclusions
defeated and no explicit guard, a legal async function whose body matches the
planner's counted-reduction shape and contains no await starts returning a
number instead of a Promise from its second call onward. Restoring one
condition at the decision point -- NumericLoopPlanner.TryCreate refuses a
suspendable function -- restores correct answers with both exclusions still
defeated. What is left in that configuration is the generic numericPlan == null
re-compile, which speculates on nothing and cannot violate the contract.

Adds RestartContractTests (16 cases): the three suspendable shapes with the
matching body are never tiered and keep returning their objects, with the same
body as an ordinary function tiered as the control; a yielding generator still
iterates; a deoptimizing call produces the same number of observable effects as
the untiered engine, counted through a valueOf on the ARGUMENT because adding a
statement to the body would stop it being tiered and the test would pass
vacuously; every guard answers what an untiered JSContext answers; and the
bailout unwinds from 200 frames deep and stays catchable.

This states and enforces the contract the pilot already runs under. It does not
widen what may be speculated on; speculation inside a body still needs 4-3b.
